          "id": "sec-1-10",
          "secNumber": "1.10",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Fluid Properties, Non-Newtonian Rheology and Shear Response",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Fluid properties \u2014 assessment\n\n**Instructions:** Each answer must include a physical explanation and dimensional check. Use the corresponding interactive laboratories for exploration, not as substitute proof. The questions are intended to test derivation, model choice, diagnostic reasoning and quantitative sensitivity.\n\n\n### Topic 1: Density and specific gravity\n\n1. Starting with `\u03c1=m/V; SG=\u03c1/\u03c1ref`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **estimate mass flow entering an oil blending train from a measured volumetric feed and a density sample.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Mixing weight and mass units produces a systematic design error; a density value with no thermal state is incomplete. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **absolute pressure (bar)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 2: Newtonian viscosity and temperature\n\n1. Starting with `\u03c4=\u03bc(du/dy); \u03bd=\u03bc/\u03c1`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **compare friction in water at two operating temperatures and check whether the intended pump remains in its useful range.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Using viscosity in centipoise as if it were pascal seconds changes Reynolds number by a factor of one thousand. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **shear rate (s\u207b\u00b9)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 3: Bulk modulus and fluid compressibility\n\n1. Starting with `K=\u2212V(dp/dV); c\u2248sqrt(K/\u03c1)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **consider a chemical transfer line that experiences a rapid emergency isolation.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Confusing isothermal and adiabatic compressibility leads to an incorrect estimated wave speed. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **absolute pressure (bar)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 4: Non-Newtonian flow curves\n\n1. Starting with `\u03c4=K\u03b3\u0307\u207f; \u03bcapp=K\u03b3\u0307\u207f\u207b\u00b9`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **investigate a concentrated food paste transferred between a jacketed vessel and a packaging line.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Substituting one apparent viscosity into a Newtonian Moody diagram is not automatically valid. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **shear rate (s\u207b\u00b9)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n## Scoring guidance\n\nAward 30% for correct physical modeling and regimes, 25% for quantitative steps and units, 25% for interpretation and sensitivity, and 20% for validation, evidence and honest acknowledgment of limitations. The open diagnostic questions deliberately admit more than one defensible answer; unsupported claims receive no credit."
