          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Fluid Statics, Hydrostatic Pressure Fields and Manometry",
          "content": "# Unit 2: Flow balances \u2014 assessment\n\n**Instructions:** Each answer must include a physical explanation and dimensional check. Use the corresponding interactive laboratories for exploration, not as substitute proof. The questions are intended to test derivation, model choice, diagnostic reasoning and quantitative sensitivity.\n\n\n### Topic 1: Continuity across equipment\n\n1. Starting with `\u1e41=\u03c1Av; at steady state \u03a3\u1e41in=\u03a3\u1e41out`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **size a constricted process-line spool while preserving the required mass throughput.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. A junction may have a new density if temperature or phase changes; equal volume flows do not always mean equal mass flows. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **diameter (mm)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 2: Momentum and anchor forces\n\n1. Starting with `\u03a3F=\u03a3out\u1e41v\u2212\u03a3in\u1e41v`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **estimate the force developed by a 90-degree bend in a cooling-water circuit.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Ignoring pressure thrust on inlet and outlet control surfaces produces an incorrect pipe-support reaction. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **mean velocity (m/s)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 3: Mechanical-energy equation\n\n1. Starting with `p/(\u03c1g)+v\u00b2/(2g)+z+hp\u2212ht\u2212hL=constant`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **compute the discharge pressure needed for a transfer from a tank farm to a raised reactor.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Pressure head is not interchangeable with pressure in pascals; multiplying by \u03c1g is essential. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **flow rate (L/s)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 4: Irreversible dissipation\n\n1. Starting with `hL=(fL/D+\u03a3K)v\u00b2/(2g)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **prepare a pressure-loss register for a recirculation circuit with elbows and a partially open valve.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Mixing Fanning and Darcy friction factors introduces a factor-of-four discrepancy. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **loss coefficient \u03a3K (\u2014)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n## Scoring guidance\n\nAward 30% for correct physical modeling and regimes, 25% for quantitative steps and units, 25% for interpretation and sensitivity, and 20% for validation, evidence and honest acknowledgment of limitations. The open diagnostic questions deliberately admit more than one defensible answer; unsupported claims receive no credit."
