          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 08: Parallel piping flow split",
          "content": "# Laboratory 08: Parallel piping flow split\n\n## Purpose and governing physics\n\n**Primary observation:** Flow in branch 1 (L/s). **Equation:** `Equal branch head loss; Q1+Q2=Qtotal`.\n\nFlow split is computed from idealized resistance coefficients. This model uses constant turbulent f=0.025, not iterative Colebrook. The model uses explicit units and a clearly selected control variable, rather than displaying an arbitrary animation. Examine it as a *hypothesis*: it is an engineering relationship with assumptions, not a guarantee for every real material or installation.\n\n## Before interacting\n\n1. Identify the physically independent variable: **total flow** in **L/s**. What property or device controls it in the actual plant?\n2. Confirm that `Equal branch head loss; Q1+Q2=Qtotal` predicts a dimensionally consistent quantity. Identify any constants or coefficients that require measurement rather than theory.\n3. Predict qualitatively whether the displayed **flow in branch 1** will increase, decrease, or reach a limiting value. Record the prediction before changing the slider.\n\n## Worked baseline\n\nWith total flow = 7 L/s, the displayed model predicts **5.18315 L/s** using the other control values shown below. This is a numerical consequence of the simplified equation, not an empirical observation.\n\n| Model input | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---:|---|\n| total flow | 7 L/s | Primary independent variable |\n| D1 | 80 mm | Controlled model parameter |\n| D2 | 55 mm | Controlled model parameter |\n| L1 | 80 m | Controlled model parameter |\n| L2 | 100 m | Controlled model parameter |\n\n## Sensitivity experiment\n\nHold all other controls fixed and examine the output at approximately 15% and 80% of the horizontal-axis range. The first case gives **2.85073 L/s**, whereas the second gives **11.9953 L/s**. The difference demonstrates sensitivity of the equation over this range. Check for nonlinear curvature rather than inferring a constant slope from one pair of points.\n\nNow change exactly one secondary control, if available. Predict the effect first and then observe how the whole curve changes. Distinguish changes that shift an entire graph from changes that alter its slope or create a limiting value. Export the CSV to calculate a finite-difference sensitivity from two nearby points; report the input perturbation and measurement units.\n\n## Interpretation, failure modes and extension\n\nFlow split is computed from idealized resistance coefficients. This model uses constant turbulent f=0.025, not iterative Colebrook. A useful result is not only a number but a statement of the domain in which that number should be believed. If the model predicts negative flow, unattainable efficiency, or implausibly large pressure, check parameter choices, the sign convention and whether the equation has been extrapolated beyond its assumptions. Do not use the apparent precision of the displayed decimals as an estimate of actual physical accuracy.\n\nFor an engineering extension, identify one measured input and one unknown input, propose an instrument and a reasonable calibration experiment, and specify how input uncertainty would propagate into flow in branch 1. Compare the ideal trend with what plant data could look like if temperature changed, a valve fouled, or a sensor drifted. A diagram on the interactive page is schematic and not to scale.\n\n## Investigation questions\n\n1. Which assumption would first fail if this process were scaled to a plant with a tenfold larger characteristic flow or vessel size?\n2. If total flow increases but the observed flow in branch 1 moves in the opposite direction to the model, identify at least two plausible missing physical effects.\n3. Derive the dimensions of `Equal branch head loss; Q1+Q2=Qtotal` and explain why reporting the wrong viscosity, pressure basis or density could reverse a process decision.\n4. Describe a practical experiment that could distinguish model error from instrument error. State the minimum measurements required.\n\n[Open interactive model](lab-08.html)"
