          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Pumps, Compressors, Turbomachinery and Cavitation NPSH Limits",
          "content": "# Unit 5 applied project: Process equipment\n\n## Design brief\n\nA medium-size chemical plant is assessing a fluid transfer or processing subsystem. Your assigned topic is **process equipment**. The commissioning dossier contains a process sketch, a partial set of flow and pressure readings, and uncertainty in at least one fluid property. Construct a transparent calculation that can be checked independently. The aim is to evaluate available evidence, not to certify equipment for a hazardous duty.\n\n## Specific project route\n\nFluid equipment must be evaluated together with the surrounding piping. A pump curve is neither a fixed delivery flow nor a guarantee of efficiency; its intersection with the system curve establishes a possible operating point. Valves alter hydraulic resistance, and sensors return readings with calibration, installation and disturbance limitations. Compressor calculations require compressible thermodynamics rather than incompressible head analogies.\n\nBuild a measurement plan with a process flow diagram and separate mass, energy, and equipment assumptions. Choose two laboratory models related to this unit, identify which governing equations overlap and which assumptions conflict, and reconcile them by writing a joint list of state variables. Compare two plausible operating points and explain which one you would investigate first. If the calculations cannot determine a unique operating point, show explicitly which additional measured condition is missing.\n\n## Required outputs\n\n1. A process sketch showing node elevations, flow directions, measurement locations and equipment labels.\n2. A one-page derivation, with dimensions checked and at least one explicitly computed limiting case.\n3. A CSV dataset generated by changing an appropriate laboratory model input across its allowed range. Include plots with physical units.\n4. A sensitivity analysis that changes one material parameter, one boundary condition and one equipment or geometry parameter independently.\n5. A decision note that separates validated physical relationships, assumed correlations and unresolved uncertainty.\n\n## Diagnostic questions\n\nWhat observed trend would disprove the selected model? How would your process react if temperature increased while a valve became partially blocked? Could the same output change be explained by a sensor error? When would running an experiment on the real equipment be unsafe or unnecessary because the model has already violated a pressure or flow limit?\n\n**Assessment:** Technical clarity 30%; defensible equations 25%; documented calculations 20%; sensitivity and verification 15%; communication and honest scope limits 10%."
