          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Pumps, Compressors, Turbomachinery and Cavitation NPSH Limits",
          "content": "# Unit 5: Process equipment \u2014 assessment\n\n**Instructions:** Each answer must include a physical explanation and dimensional check. Use the corresponding interactive laboratories for exploration, not as substitute proof. The questions are intended to test derivation, model choice, diagnostic reasoning and quantitative sensitivity.\n\n\n### Topic 1: Centrifugal pump performance\n\n1. Starting with `Hp(Q)=H0\u2212aQ\u00b2; Hsys(Q)=Hstatic+kQ\u00b2`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **select a candidate transfer pump for an elevated process vessel and examine a fouled filter case.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Reading head from a pump curve without intersecting the system curve does not establish actual flow. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **static head (m)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 2: Pump speed and affinity scaling\n\n1. Starting with `Q\u221dN; H\u221dN\u00b2; P\u221dN\u00b3`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **estimate the energy consequence of a variable-speed retrofit in a solvent circulation loop.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Applying cubic affinity scaling to the full system energy usage without checking fixed lift is misleading. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **speed fraction N/N0 (\u2014)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 3: Control valve characteristics\n\n1. Starting with `Q\u221dCv sqrt(\u0394p/SG)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **investigate why a temperature-control valve spends most of its working time nearly shut.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. An equal-percentage stem characteristic does not guarantee equal-percentage installed flow. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **travel fraction (\u2014)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 4: Flow measurement and uncertainty\n\n1. Starting with `Q=Cd A sqrt(2\u0394p/\u03c1)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **choose an orifice meter range for variable batch-plant service.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Neglecting upstream swirl and impulse-line errors falsely attributes all disagreement to the flow transmitter. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **mean velocity (m/s)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n## Scoring guidance\n\nAward 30% for correct physical modeling and regimes, 25% for quantitative steps and units, 25% for interpretation and sensitivity, and 20% for validation, evidence and honest acknowledgment of limitations. The open diagnostic questions deliberately admit more than one defensible answer; unsupported claims receive no credit."
