          "content": "# Unit 6: Multiphase flow \u2014 assessment\n\n**Instructions:** Each answer must include a physical explanation and dimensional check. Use the corresponding interactive laboratories for exploration, not as substitute proof. The questions are intended to test derivation, model choice, diagnostic reasoning and quantitative sensitivity.\n\n\n### Topic 1: Packed-bed friction\n\n1. Starting with `\u0394p/L=150\u03bc(1\u2212\u03b5)\u00b2u/(\u03b5\u00b3dp\u00b2)+1.75\u03c1(1\u2212\u03b5)u\u00b2/(\u03b5\u00b3dp)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **estimate pressure required to circulate process gas through a catalyst bed.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Inserting the interstitial speed in a superficial-speed correlation distorts both contributions. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **superficial velocity (m/s)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 2: Fluidization onset\n\n1. Starting with `\u0394pbed\u2248(\u03c1s\u2212\u03c1f)(1\u2212\u03b5)Lg`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **predict minimum airflow needed to suspend a granular catalyst in a small test column.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. The pressure drop does not keep increasing according to the fixed-bed Ergun equation after a bed expands. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **particle size (mm)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 3: Gas\u2013liquid contacting\n\n1. Starting with `\u03b1g\u2248Qg/(Qg+Ql)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **evaluate an air-lift loop before committing to a two-phase pressure-drop design.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Assuming both phases move at equal local velocity in a slug-flow reactor can give erroneous residence times. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **superficial velocity (m/s)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 4: Slurry transport and settling\n\n1. Starting with `vsettling\u2248(\u03c1p\u2212\u03c1f)gdp\u00b2/(18\u03bc)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **screen settling risk in a mineral-processing feed pipe during a low-flow operating period.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Applying Stokes settling beyond small particle Reynolds number is a common serious mistake. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **particle size (mm)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n## Scoring guidance\n\nAward 30% for correct physical modeling and regimes, 25% for quantitative steps and units, 25% for interpretation and sensitivity, and 20% for validation, evidence and honest acknowledgment of limitations. The open diagnostic questions deliberately admit more than one defensible answer; unsupported claims receive no credit."
