          "content": "# Unit 7: Compressible flow \u2014 assessment\n\n**Instructions:** Each answer must include a physical explanation and dimensional check. Use the corresponding interactive laboratories for exploration, not as substitute proof. The questions are intended to test derivation, model choice, diagnostic reasoning and quantitative sensitivity.\n\n\n### Topic 1: Gas equations of state\n\n1. Starting with `\u03c1=pM/(ZRuT)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **compare pressure versus density along a compressed-air utility line.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Mixing gauge pressure with absolute pressure can yield dramatic gas-density errors. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **absolute pressure (bar)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 2: Isentropic nozzle expansion\n\n1. Starting with `\u1e41=Cd A p0/\u221aT0 sqrt(\u03b3/R) \u03a8(pb/p0)`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **estimate an upper bound on a process gas discharge through an idealized restriction.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. Using the incompressible orifice equation at a large gas pressure ratio ignores choking. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **back-pressure ratio pb/p0 (\u2014)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 3: Gas line pressure losses\n\n1. Starting with `p1\u00b2\u2212p2\u00b2\u2248f(L/D)(\u1e41\u00b2 RT/(M A\u00b2))`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **examine an instrument-air line with a sharp downstream pressure requirement.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. A gas pipeline is not characterized by one density or one Reynolds number when pressure varies significantly. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **back-pressure ratio pb/p0 (\u2014)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n### Topic 4: Choked-flow limits and recovery\n\n1. Starting with `p*/p0=(2/(\u03b3+1))^(\u03b3/(\u03b3\u22121))`, define each quantity and derive the expected SI dimensions. If the result is not dimensionally consistent, identify which conversion is likely missing.\n2. For the practical setting, **compare small inert-gas restrictors for a purge line.**, list the minimum measurements required for a credible baseline calculation. Which input would you vary first in a sensitivity analysis and why?\n3. A choked mass-flow equation should not be extrapolated as a general piping or safety-valve rating. Explain the consequence of this error and design a specific falsification test using two independent observations.\n4. The associated laboratory varies **back-pressure ratio pb/p0 (\u2014)**. If that input changes by 20%, will the output change by exactly 20%? Demonstrate using two numerical evaluations; note whether the relationship is linear in the studied interval.\n\n## Scoring guidance\n\nAward 30% for correct physical modeling and regimes, 25% for quantitative steps and units, 25% for interpretation and sensitivity, and 20% for validation, evidence and honest acknowledgment of limitations. The open diagnostic questions deliberately admit more than one defensible answer; unsupported claims receive no credit."
